The "reverse traveling wave" pattern What you're observing is most likely broadband noise floor variance — at this power level, the analyzer's own thermal noise and ADC quantization produce a visually undulating pattern that can appear to move directionally between sweeps. It is not typically indicative of a coherent modulated signal unless a consistent carrier peak rises clearly above the noise floor by at least 10–15 dB.

To confirm or rule out a real signal:

    Switch to Max Hold mode — a real signal will accumulate above the noise over multiple sweeps
    Narrow the span to 100 kHz centered on 37.83 MHz to see if a carrier resolves
    Check the demodulation mode (AM/FM) — a real transmission at this frequency would be audible

