Can a nanovna V2 be used to measure reflection noise to determine if that is what's happening?
Apr 5

Yes, a NanoVNA V2 can absolutely be used here — and it's actually a smart diagnostic choice. Here's how it applies to your situation:

What the NanoVNA V2 measures The NanoVNA V2 measures S-parameters — specifically S11 (return loss / reflection coefficient) and S21 (transmission). S11 directly tells you how much signal is being reflected back from a device under test, expressed as a reflection coefficient or VSWR.

How this helps diagnose your 37–38 MHz observation

If the "traveling wave" pattern is caused by impedance mismatch reflection between your antenna feedline and the analyzer input, the NanoVNA can confirm this by:

    Sweeping your antenna across 30–50 MHz and plotting S11
    A poor match (high VSWR) at 37–38 MHz would mean significant reflected energy is creating standing wave patterns — which can produce exactly the kind of undulating, apparently-directional noise floor behavior you're seeing
    A well-matched antenna at that frequency would show S11 below −10 dB, meaning the noise floor is real received energy, not reflection artifact

Specific test procedure

    Calibrate the NanoVNA V2 with your SOL (Short-Open-Load) calibration kit at the measurement plane
    Connect your antenna feedline to Port 1 (CH0)
    Sweep 30–45 MHz, log the S11 Smith chart and return loss plot
    Look for resonances or anti-resonances near 37.8 MHz — a sharp dip in return loss at that frequency means your antenna is resonant there and actively receiving, confirming the signal is real

What a mismatch reflection artifact looks like vs. a real signal

    Mismatch artifact: S11 will show poor return loss (above −6 dB) at that band, and Max Hold on the spectrum analyzer will NOT accumulate a stable peak
    Real received signal: S11 shows decent match, and Max Hold DOES build a persistent peak above the noise

One limitation to note The NanoVNA V2 measures reflection of your own injected stimulus signal — it doesn't receive ambient RF the way your spectrum analyzer does. So it characterizes your antenna system's behavior, not the external signal itself. Used together, the two instruments give you a complete picture.

