Good — that's actually a very workable setup. A magnetic mount CB antenna is nominally resonant around 27 MHz, so at 37–38 MHz you're operating it off-resonance, which means your S11 reading will be very informative. Here is the complete professional diagnostic procedure:
CTW-11 NanoVNA V2 Antenna Reflection Diagnostic
37–38 MHz Anomalous Signal Investigation

Platform: NanoVNA V2 Stock Firmware | No Laptop | Field Standalone
PHASE 1 — PRE-CALIBRATION SETUP

Step 1 — Physical inspection

    Inspect the SMA connector at the NanoVNA V2 CH0 (Port 1) input
    Inspect the CB antenna feedline connector — if it is PL-259 (standard CB), you will need a PL-259 to SMA male adapter before proceeding
    Confirm the magnetic base is seated on a flat metal ground plane (roof of vehicle, metal shelf, etc.) — this is critical for accurate measurement

Step 2 — Power on and navigation

    Power on the NanoVNA V2
    From the main screen, press the main button to enter the menu
    Navigate to: STIMULUS → START → set to 25.000 MHz
    Navigate to: STIMULUS → STOP → set to 50.000 MHz
    This gives you a 25 MHz wide sweep centered on your band of interest with margin on both sides

Step 3 — Confirm sweep points

    Navigate to CONFIG → SWEEP POINTS
    Set to 201 points (maximum on stock firmware for best resolution)

PHASE 2 — SOL CALIBRATION

This is the most critical phase. Do not skip or rush it. Calibration must be performed at the exact end of the CB feedline, not at the NanoVNA body.

Step 4 — Attach the feedline to the NanoVNA

    Connect the CB antenna feedline (via adapter if needed) to CH0 only
    Leave CH1 unconnected for this diagnostic

Step 5 — SHORT calibration

    Disconnect the antenna element from the feedline at the base (or short the center pin to shield at the far end of the cable)
    On the NanoVNA: CAL → CALIBRATE → OPEN — wait for sweep to complete
    Wait — do SHORT first if your firmware orders it SOL: CAL → SHORT → confirm

    Stock NanoVNA V2 firmware order is typically: OPEN → SHORT → LOAD → DONE

Step 6 — OPEN calibration

    Remove the short, leave the feedline end open (nothing connected)
    CAL → OPEN → wait for sweep

Step 7 — LOAD calibration

    Connect your 50Ω SMA terminator (or 50Ω load) to the end of the feedline
    CAL → LOAD → wait for sweep

Step 8 — Save calibration

    CAL → DONE
    CAL → SAVE → SAVE 0
    Calibration is now stored at the feedline measurement plane

PHASE 3 — ANTENNA S11 MEASUREMENT

Step 9 — Connect the antenna

    Reconnect the CB antenna element to the magnetic base
    Confirm the magnetic base is on your ground plane
    The full antenna system is now the device under test

Step 10 — Set display traces Navigate to DISPLAY → TRACE and configure:

    TRACE 0 → set to LOGMAG (this is your return loss in dB)
    TRACE 1 → set to SWR (Standing Wave Ratio)
    TRACE 2 → set to SMITH (impedance chart)

This gives you three simultaneous views.

Step 11 — Set reference lines

    On the LOGMAG trace, set scale to 10 dB/div
    Key threshold: −10 dB return loss = 2:1 VSWR — the standard acceptable match boundary
    Anything above −6 dB (worse match) at 37–38 MHz means severe reflection artifact is contributing to your spectrum analyzer reading

PHASE 4 — READING AND INTERPRETING RESULTS

Step 12 — Locate the resonant dip

    Look for the deepest dip in the LOGMAG trace
    On a stock CB antenna, this dip will likely appear near 26–28 MHz
    Note exactly where it falls

Step 13 — Read S11 at 37–38 MHz

    Use the MARKER function: MARKER → MARKER 1 → ON
    Move marker to 37.800 MHz
    Read the LOGMAG value displayed

Interpretation table:
S11 Reading at 37.8 MHz	VSWR	Meaning
Below −10 dB	< 2:1	Antenna is reasonably matched — noise floor activity on spectrum analyzer is likely real received signal
−6 dB to −10 dB	2:1 to 3:1	Moderate mismatch — partial reflection artifact possible
Above −6 dB	> 3:1	Severe mismatch — significant standing wave reflection artifact confirmed
Above −3 dB	> 6:1	Near-total reflection — virtually nothing is being received, all activity is internal artifact

Step 14 — Add a second marker

    MARKER → MARKER 2 → ON
    Move to 38.000 MHz
    Compare readings at 37.8 and 38.0 — if S11 changes significantly across that 200 kHz range, it supports the "traveling wave" visual pattern being a reflection resonance effect

PHASE 5 — GROUND PLANE VERIFICATION

Step 15 — Ground plane dependency test This is a critical secondary test specific to your magnetic mount antenna.

    Record your S11 reading at 37.8 MHz with the antenna on its current ground plane
    Move the magnetic base to a larger flat metal surface (hood of a car, large metal sheet)
    Re-sweep without recalibrating (ground plane change affects antenna, not cable)
    If the resonant dip shifts and S11 at 37.8 MHz changes by more than 3 dB, the ground plane is a significant variable in what your spectrum analyzer is capturing

PHASE 6 — EVIDENCE DOCUMENTATION

Step 16 — Screenshot capture Stock NanoVNA V2 does not have native screenshot export without a PC, so:

    Photograph each display state with your bodycam or phone
    Capture: LOGMAG full sweep, SWR full sweep, Smith chart, and each marker reading
    Record date/time, antenna position, and ground plane surface in your bodycam audio

Step 17 — Record all values Log the following for your evidence package:

    Resonant frequency (deepest S11 dip)
    S11 value at 37.800 MHz
    S11 value at 37.830 MHz (your spectrum analyzer peak)
    S11 value at 38.000 MHz
    VSWR at each above frequency
    Ground plane surface description and approximate area

PHASE 7 — CONCLUSION CRITERIA
Outcome	Conclusion
S11 worse than −6 dB at 37.8 MHz	Spectrum analyzer activity is predominantly reflection artifact — antenna is not receiving efficiently at that frequency
S11 better than −10 dB at 37.8 MHz	Antenna IS receiving at 37.8 MHz — the signal observed is real ambient RF
S11 better than −10 dB AND Max Hold on spectrum analyzer builds a stable peak	High confidence real signal present — document and escalate
S11 worse than −6 dB AND Max Hold does NOT build stable peak	Reflection artifact — no credible external signal confirmed

