Quick question, what's the ground plane?
Apr 6

Simple answer — in antenna theory, the ground plane is the conductive surface that acts as the electrical "mirror" or reference for the antenna's radiating element.

For your magnetic mount CB antenna specifically:

The antenna has two halves electrically — the vertical whip (the springy rod you can see) and an invisible half which is the mirror image that the ground plane substitutes for. The magnetic base exists purely to make solid electrical contact with whatever metal surface it's sitting on, and that metal surface becomes the ground plane.

In practical terms for your setup:

    Car roof = large, excellent ground plane
    Metal shelf = decent ground plane
    Wood table = no ground plane at all — the antenna is essentially broken electrically
    Concrete floor = no ground plane
    Holding it in your hand = poor, unstable ground plane

Why it matters for your diagnostic: A CB antenna with no proper ground plane or a small/marginal one will have its resonant frequency shift unpredictably and its S11 will look terrible across the board — meaning your spectrum analyzer would show noise floor artifacts regardless of whether a real signal exists.

